  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "Can checking only that the proposition holds for \\(n=1,2,3,4\\) replace mathematical induction?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "Yes, 4 examples are enough",
    "B": "No, finitely many examples do not constitute a proof",
    "C": "Yes, as long as they are all positive",
    "D": "No, because induction does not allow verification of the starting point"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Induction uses the inductive step to cover all positive integers after the starting point. Checking just a few terms is insufficient.\n\nAlternative method 1: A statement that fails at 5\n\nConsider the statement that every positive integer \\(n\\) satisfies \\(n\\le4\\). It is true for \\(n=1,2,3,4\\), but false for \\(n=5\\). Thus four successful checks cannot prove a statement for every positive integer. The answer is B.",
